          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "12.6.4 Kingdom Plantae — Multicellular, autotrophic"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.109,
            0.415,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "eukaryotes with a cell wall"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.131,
            0.775,
            0.233
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Plants are multicellular, autotrophic organisms that perform photosynthesis. Their cells have a rigid cell wall primarily made up of cellulose, which provides support and protection. All plants belong to the kingdom Plantae. Plants form the base of most of the food chains and release oxygen which is essential for life on the Earth. Kingdom Plantae is further divided into five classes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.231,
            0.919,
            0.321
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.112,
                0.231,
                0.919,
                0.321
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_9_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.333,
            0.51,
            0.352
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thallophyta (algae) — Primitive plants"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.354,
            0.776,
            0.457
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Thallophytes (thallos means undifferentiated body and phyton means plant) are among the simplest plant forms and are mostly found in water or moist environments. They form a thallus, a simple body that allows direct exchange of gases, nutrients and water with the surroundings. They are well-adapted to aquatic habitats. An example of Thallophyta is Spirogyra (Fig. 12.9)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.104,
            0.466,
            0.162,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": [
